import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A1/v0"] = """Problem: In MVFA's zero-shot (leave-one-modality-out) training, only the segmentation adapters produce the anomaly maps, and the released code trains them with Dice and Focal losses exclusively on the three source datasets that have pixel masks (BrainMRI, LiverCT, RESC); the image-labelled datasets (OCT17, ChestXray, HIS) only train the detection adapters. Can the segmentation adapters be trained from image-level labels alone — replacing the Dice and Focal terms with a multiple-instance objective on each level's patch-to-text anomaly map (an image-level BCE on the top-k mean of patch anomaly probabilities, k = 1% of patches, so that an abnormal image must contain a few strongly abnormal patches and a normal image none) applied to all five source datasets with every mask discarded — and how much of the mask-supervised localisation (pixel-level AUROC and AUPRO) and detection (image-level AUROC) on the held-out modality does it retain? The comparison is mask-supervised MVFA versus image-label-supervised MVFA, trained and model-selected under the shared protocol.

Rationale: Limitations L3, L5 and L18, checked against the released code, show that MVFA's localisation depends on dense expert masks from three datasets and that half of the benchmark never teaches the segmentation branch anything; L4 shows the same dependence in the few-shot mode (K abnormal images with masks). Image-level labels are cheap and exist for every BMAD dataset, but MVFA never uses them to supervise where anomalies are. The related literature does not answer whether text-anchored patch similarities inside a multi-level CLIP adapter can learn localisation from weak labels: MedSAD-CLIP [40] and MLCA [58] keep pixel-level supervision, MadCLIP [29] and the consistency-margin adapter [54] still rely on labelled abnormal images, MediCLIP [22], AnomaMed [53] and the UWF framework [43] replace real labels with synthetic anomalies, and weak image-level supervision has been used for separate generative models (diffusion with classifier guidance [10]) rather than for the adapter's own maps. The question changes only the training signal of one branch, is implemented by replacing the segmentation loss in the public training script, and runs within one GPU budget; a negative result still quantifies what masks are worth for cross-modality localisation, which is exactly the annotation cost that L18 says is never measured."""
A["stage1/problem/B1/v0"] = """Problem: In MVFA's few-shot inference, the released code min-max normalises the zero-shot (text) branch and the few-shot (memory-bank) branch with the minimum and maximum over the entire test set and adds them with a fixed weight of 0.5; the image score is then the maximum of the fused pixel map. The score of one image, and therefore the reported AUROC, depends on which other images are in the test set, and the scorer cannot be applied to a single incoming image. Can this transductive normalisation be replaced by an inductive, per-image one that normalises each branch with statistics computed only from the K normal support images (leave-one-out scores of each support against the remaining supports' memory bank and against the text anchors), keeping the fusion weight and the max pooling unchanged, and at what cost in image-level and pixel-level AUROC? Outcomes per held-out BMAD dataset and K in {2, 4, 8, 16}: (i) the spread of the original method's AUROC across random test subsets and at shifted prevalence (10% and 50% abnormal), (ii) image- and pixel-level AUROC of inductive versus transductive normalisation, and (iii) sensitivity at 95% specificity and expected calibration error at a threshold fixed from the support scores alone.

Rationale: The code check turned L10 into a sharper limitation (C1): the fusion is computed with test-set extremes, which makes the method non-deployable on a stream of images and makes its numbers depend on test-set composition; L8 adds that only threshold-free AUROC is reported, so this dependence is invisible. Related work changes representations, prompts and fusion layers (LaRP-CLIP's adaptive fusion [57], HeadCLIP's joint score [17], ClipMPCAD [62]) but evaluates with the same AUROC; QueCo [56] goes further and builds its reference from the unlabelled test set itself, while CANOR [30] shows that a handful of calibration scalars can be learnt cheaply for a frozen CLIP. MVFA already loads K normal support images per modality but never uses them for normalisation, so an inductive normaliser needs no new data and no retraining: the change is confined to the scoring code (test_few.py) and runs on the released checkpoints retrained under the shared protocol. Keeping the pooling fixed isolates one component; the pooling question (L38, C3) can then be studied separately on top of a per-image score."""
json.dump(A, open("/home/claude/task3/logs/answers/run3/s1_v0.json", "w"), indent=1)
